  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 429 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThis evening through Friday night...\n\nMain concern for this evening on through tomorrow night\ncontinues to lie with precipitation chances. Models remain in \ngood agreement in the broader picture, showing continued \nsouthwesterly flow in the upper levels...our precipitation \nchances driven by the passage of a couple more shortwave \ndisturbances. After a bit of a lull from activity which moved in\nlast night...the next round of showers/storms is already \nworking its way into the region from the southwest. Models \ncontinue to show this evening/overnight being the main timeframe\nwith this next batch...which they also show being more \nscattered in nature (vs the widespread shield of precip we had \nearlier in the day). Overall threat for severe weather is \nlow...but instability isn't zero, not out of the question a few \nstorms through tonight could produce some small hail/gusty \nwinds. Of more concern would be additional rainfall...if any \nstronger storms with more notable rates were to spend much time \nover areas which already had 2- 4 inches of rain from earlier \ntoday, could have some minor flooding issues arise. \n\nDuring the daytime hours on Friday...in general models have\ntrended toward a less active period. Can't totally rule out \nprecipitation for any particular portion of the forecast \narea...but the coverage appears that it'll be more isolated-\nscattered in nature. Better chances look to arrive once again \nlater in the day and more into the evening/overnight hours as \nlift increases with the arrival of the next upper level \nshortwave disturbance. Again the overall threat for severe \nweather is low (SPC Day 2 remains 'general thunder' for the \narea)...but if can get a few breaks in the clouds/better \ninstability into the area, can't rule out some stronger storms.\n\nThis weekend on into the new work week...\n\nStill some uncertainty with just how quickly precipitation exits\nthe forecast area off to the east on Saturday...some models \nhave continued to trend a touch slower, potentially lingering \nactivity across eastern areas well into the afternoon hours. \nOnce the upper level trough driving those chances pushes east, \nmodels showing shortwave ridging working its way across the \nPlains...drying the forecast for Saturday night through at least\nSunday. It's possible that much of Monday ends up being dry as \nwell...but do have some low chance PoPs in the forecast. Models \nshowing the next notable upper level system arriving in the \nTuesday-ish time frame...but uncertainties remain due to \ndifferences between models with how the system moves through. \nThe ECMWF remains on the slower/stronger side with the potential\nfor more preciptiation...the GFS more progressive/quicker. \nPanning out like the ECMWF would drag precipitation chances \nfurther out into the rest of the week...GFS dries things out \nquicker. As far as temperatures go...still looking at 70s-low \n80s for the weekend-Mon...potentially more 60s Tuesday on, \ndepending on how precip works out.",
